## 1. Introduction
Natural polymers have been widely used for the production of biomaterials such as scaffolds, drug delivery systems, and wound dressings [1–3] due to their biocompatibility, biodegradability, bioactivity, low cost, and abundance in nature [4–8]. A popular example of the natural polymers is pectin, which is an anionic hetero-polysaccharide from terrestrial plant tissues [9,10]. Pectin is composed of smooth homogalacturonan (HG) domains and hairy rhamnogalacturonan (RG) domains containing (1, 4)- $\mathbf{\alpha}\cdot\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\alpha}^{}$ -linked galacturonic acid which can be methyl esterified in the C-6 position or non-esterified [11–13]. Because of its naturally esterified galacturonic acid units, pectin exhibited strong anti-inflammatory capability by inhibiting enzymes such as inducible nitric oxide synthase (iNOS) and cyclooxygenase 2 (COX-2) [14] reducing potential irritation like other polysaccharide based hydrogels. In addition, pectin proceeds cell signaling, adhesion, proliferation, and differentiation [15]. Combination of rich biological activity and ionic cross-linkable property, make pectin an interesting candidate material for biomedical applications such as drug delivery, wound healing, and bone regeneration [16–21].
Each year approximately 5,000,000 people experience severe skin injuries demanding quick wound management based on the World Health Organization (WHO) reports [15]. In general, wounds, if do not treated properly in 90 days, lead to chronic wounds contributing to increased healthcare costs and often surgical treatment [17,22,23]. In this regard, synthesis of a drug delivery system which can be used as a wound dressing instantaneously could develop a promising strategy to address this problem. Because, it facilitates the effective delivery of pharmaceuticals or biologics at the proper dosage and time into the wound bed [23]. Electrospun polymeric nano-fibrous mats have intrinsic porous fibrous structure mimicking the fibrils of extracellular matrices (ECMs) [4,22], high specific surface area leading to enhanced interactions with their surrounding medium [19,24], and controllable fiber size [16]. This is the reason why they meet numerous desired requirements of an ideal wound dressing [9,22,25,26] and controlled release systems [5,25,27,28]. When developing a hybrid controlled release systems, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), especially Nanoscale MOFs (nanoMOFs), have been shown to be well suited as nanocarriers for organic drug or metallodrug delivery [29]. MOFs known as porous coordination polymers are a new class of hybrid dimensionally by poly-functional organic ligands with short- and long-range order. MOFs have a high surface area $(1000{-}7000~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ), tunable pore shape and size due to diverse coordination and organic functionalities, and good thermal stability. These unique properties nominate MOFs as a promising candidate for gas adsorption [30] and separation [31], catalysis, luminescence, sensing, electrochemical biomedical applications [32–35]. For example, Zhao et al. loaded the extract of red chili into $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{III}}}$ doped hollow metal-organic frameworks incorporated in gelatin/chitosan film to develop antibacterial biomaterials for food packaging [36]. Recently, biological metal organic frameworks (bio-MOFs) have emerged from the combination of MOF chemistry and bioscience as a green sustainable framework. There are two distinctive viewpoints about Bio–MOFs as reported in the literatures: (i) In the construction of bio-MOFs, biologically derived biomolecules are incorporated as a building unit of MOFs. (ii) MOFs are often used in sector of biology and medicine due to their high porosity [37,38]. Many researchers studied MOFs for drug delivery. Jiang et al. constructed the first Zr-based MOF in which anionic drugs exhibit reverse pH response for oral drug delivery [39]. Lin et al. prepared A biocompatible metal– organic framework Zn-GA comprising zinc ions and t L-glutamic acid having controlled drug release triggered by pH and thermal stimuli as a smart drug delivery system for cancer therapy [40]. Javanbakht et al. proposed a drug delivery system for the colonic administration of 5- fluorouracil (5-FU) as an anticancer drug. 5-FU was encapsulated into the Zn-based metal-organic framework (MOF-5) and subsequently protected by carboxymethyl cellulose (CMC) biopolymer [41]. MOF199 known also as Hkust-1, is analogous to metal organic frameworks, composed of dimeric cupric tetracarboxylate units where each copper ion has been coordinated with four benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylic acid (BTC) as a tritopic linker [32,33]. Copper (Cu) ions are antimicrobial agents involving in many wound-healing-related processes. It induces vascular endothelial growth factor, angiogenesis, and the expression and stabilization of extracellular skin proteins, such as keratin and collagen [32,42]. However, elevated non-physiological concentrations of copper ions increase the risk of toxicity, because they can interfere with the homeostasis of other metals, damage DNA, and generate reactive oxygen species that can adversely modify proteins, lipids, and nucleic acids. The oral lethal dose (LD50) of copper ions is $25\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ (body weight) [29] and the toxicity rating of copper ions is class 3 (moderately toxic) on the Hodge and Sterner Scale [43]. Incorporation of copper ions in some systems which delivers them slowly without any burst release at the desired location could be a solution to alleviate its toxicity [32,42]. Hkust-1 shows low cytotoxicity in vitro [32,33] but it has limited stability in protein solutions. Thus, folic acid is used as a stabilizer to synthesize a Bio–MOF (called F-Hkust in this study). Folic acid, also known as vitamin B9, refers to the synthetic, oxidized form of folate which cannot be synthesized by the human body [44–46]. Specifically, the health benefits of folic acid have attracted researchers' attention considerably over the past decade due to its classical role in important biosynthetic pathways such as some amino acids syntheses and in maintaining the normal cell growth and replication processes. Recently, Its epigenetic role also has been found [45]. Insufficient folic acid intake caused impaired normal growth and development, neural tube defects, DNA instability, cardiovascular diseases, and various cancers [47]. It is a biocompatible compound preventing water molecules from clustering in the pores and subsequently breaking the metal–carboxylate linkages of the Cu based MOFs due to its chemical groups. The intrinsic instability of Hkust-1 towards hydrolysis enables binding of folic acid molecules to the framework structure [42]. In this article, we incorporated F-Hkusts in pectin/PEO nanofibers to reduce Cu ions and folic acid burst release to develop antibacterial properties of the nanofibrous mat as well as promote fibroblast proliferation [42]. We compared pectin/PEO/FHkust composite nanofibers with different proportions of F-Hkust in terms of morphology, mechanical properties, antibacterial activity, and cytotoxicity (Fig. 1). To our knowledge, there are no published reports on the simultaneous controlled release of copper ions and folic acid from pectin nanofibers using nanoMOF frame structure.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
Citrus pectin (galacturonic acid $\ge74.0\%$ ), Poly ethylene oxide (PEO) (Molecular weight (Mw) of 2000 & $4000~\mathrm{kDa}$ ), Copper (II) acetate monohydrate $\geq98.0\%$ purity), 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid (Trimesic Acid, $98\%$ purity), calcium chloride dehydrate $(\mathsf{C a C l}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ ion as a crosslinker), and Folic acid $297\%$ purity) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich, USA. PEO $\mathrm{'Mw}=1000\mathrm{kDa}$ ) was provided from Aladdin, Shanghai, China. Triton X-100 and glacial acetic acid were supplied by Emd, USA and Acros, USA, respectively. Dimethyl Sulfoxide (DMSO, $99.9\%$ purity), glutaraldehyde (GLU) and hydrochloric acid (HCl, $36.5\substack{-38.0\%}$ (w/w) purity) were obtained from Merck, Germany. All reagents were used as received without further purification and all aqueous solutions were prepared with double distilled water.
## 2.2. Synthesis of Hkust-1 and F-Hkust
Cu-based MOF referred as “Hkust-1” degrade in physiological protein-containing solutions rapidly, restraining their direct use in biomedical applications [42]. To overcome this limitation, Hkust-1 can be increased at hydrophobicity; hence, it is modified with a small biological molecule that is sparingly soluble in water, folic acid. The chemical groups of folic acid are $\mathfrak{p}$ -aminobenzoic acid, pteridine, and glutamic acid causing its solubility in water at neutral pH. Thus, folic acid can be added during Hkust-1 synthesis to generate F-Hkust [42,44].
Hkust-1 and F-Hkust were synthesized as previously described with few modifications [42]. Firstly, $26.1~\mathrm{mg}$ of Folic acid was dissolved in $36~\mathrm{mL}$ DMSO mixed with $2~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol containing $55~\mathrm{mg}$ Trimesic acid. Then $34~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol $50\%$ and $0.75\mathrm{~g~}$ triton $\mathbf{X}^{-100}$ were added to mentioned solution to form Solution 1. Secondly, Solution 2 was prepared by dissolving $75\mathrm{~mg~}$ copper acetate monohydrate in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water. Solution 2 was added (drop by drop) into Solution 1 stirring for $40~\mathrm{{min}}$ at room temperature to obtain green suspension. Afterward, the suspensions were centrifuged at $8000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $5\mathrm{min}$ and the precipitates were washed thrice with reaction medium DMSO/ethanol/water (2: 1:1, v/v/v) and ethanol, respectively, to obtain purified F-Hkust.
## 2.3. F-Hkust characterization
The particle size and morphology of Hkust-1 and Nano F-Hkust MOFs were examined by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and field emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) using AIS2300c (Germany) and Hitachi S4160 (Japan) microscopes, respectively. The samples were dissolved, diluted, and sonicated in distilled water. After one drop of each sample dried on aluminium foil, they were coated with a thin gold layer using sputter coater unit before microscopy. Existing functional groups and intermolecular interactions of Hkust-1 and F-Hkust were determined by Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) analysis (PerkinElmer, USA). The test was conducted using the potassium bromide (KBr) pellet method, in which the prepared Hkust-1 and F-Hkust were grounded. Then Hkust-1 and FHkust were mixed with KBr followed by molding into a disc. The spectra of samples were determined over the range of $400{-}4000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Sonicated F-Hkust solution in methanol was prepared for dynamic light scattering (DLS) and Zeta potential testing to determine the size distribution as well as surface charge of Nano F-Hkust (SZ-100z, Horiba Ltd., UK). Hkust-1 and F-Hkust were characterized by X-ray diffraction analysis (XRD) analysis using a PHILIPS X'Pert MPD diffractometer (Netherlands) equipped with Cu rotating anode X-ray source $\uplambda=$ 1.54 Å). Measurements were recorded in reflection mode with diffracted beam monochromator using a step scan mode with the step of $0.05^{\circ}$ (2θ) and 1 s per step $(5\mathrm{-}60^{\circ})$ .
## 2.4. Electrospinning of pectin/PEO
Electrospinning of different pectin/PEO ratios and Mws of PEO were conducted in order that the best fitted nanofibers were chosen to be loaded by F-Hkust MOFs. In this regard, $4\mathrm{wt\%}$ pectin solutions and 4 wt% PEO solutions were prepared in water containing $2\%(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{v/v}})$ acetic acid separately and mixed to give the desired pectin/PEO mass ratio.
Moreover, the PEO with the Mws of 1000, 2000 and $4000\mathrm{kDa}$ (named as PEO1000, PEO2000, and PEO4000) were introduced into pectin solution to optimize nanofibers in terms of morphology and mechanical properties. Triton X-100 also was added to the mixture at the concentration of 1 wt% [11]. After that, the solutions were stirred for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ and stored overnight before electrospinning. The polymer solutions were placed into a $10\mathrm{mL}$ syringe capped with a 20-gauge stainless steel needle. A DC voltage of $17{-}20\mathrm{kV}$ with low current output was applied. The distance between syringe needle tip and the drum set at $18\mathrm{cm}$ . The relative humidity was in the range of $30\text{\textperthousand}$ .
## 2.5. Pectin/PEO characterization
## 2.5.1. Morphological and chemical characterization
The morphologies of the nanofibers were evaluated using SEM (Hitachi, Japan). The average diameters of the nanofibers were reported calculating the statistical average value of 50 nanofibers in the SEM pictures by Image J software. FTIR and attenuated Fourier transform infrared (ATR-FTIR) spectroscopy was conducted on pure pectin and pectin-based mats, respectively (Agilent Technologies, USA).
## 2.5.2. Mechanical behavior study
To assess tensile mechanical properties of the pectin mats and obtained composite membranes, the nanofibrous mats were cut into $40\:\mathrm{mm}\times5\:\mathrm{mm}$ rectangular specimens. The specimens were mounted onto the mechanical testing machine, equipped with a data acquisition system (5566, Instron, USA). After that, the rectangular-shaped mats were subjected to static tensile at a tensile rate of $10\ \mathrm{mm}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , followed standard mechanical testing methods for fabric textiles. This test repeated five times for each sample. The Young's modulus (E), ultimate tensile strength $(\sigma)$ , and ultimate tensile strain at break (ε) were calculated from the load (N) vs. strain curves using specific weights. The specific strength $(S.\sigma)$ was obtained using the following equation [48]:
$$
\upsigma_{\mathrm{specific}}\left(\mathbf{N_{\alpha}}\mathrm{tex}^{-1}\right)=\frac{\mathrm{load}\left(N\right)/\mathrm{width}\left(m m\right)}{\mathrm{Areal}\mathrm{density}\left(g m^{-2}\right)}
$$
where the areal density is the mass of the test specimen divided by its area, calculated by following equation:
## 2.6. Electrospinning of pectin/PEO/F-Hkust
Firstly, 0, 1, and $2\mathrm{wt\%}$ of solutions F-Hkust were added into $2\:\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}\%$ acetic acid followed by sonication. Then $100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of PEO was added into the solution and stirred about $6{-}8\mathrm{h}$ till PEO was completely dissolved. After that, pectin and Triton X-100 were added to the mixture at the amounts provided in Table 1. The ratio of pectin to PEO was 3:1 (w/ w) in all sample solutions. The solution was stirred for other $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ to become homogenous. The final polymer solution was loaded into a $5\mathrm{mL}$ syringe capped with a 20-gauge stainless steel needle. A DC voltage of $14{-}15\mathrm{kV}$ was applied at a spinning distance of $15\mathrm{cm}$ between the syringe needle tip and the ground electrode at a constant flow rate of $0.5\mathrm{mLh}^{-1}$ . Pectin/PEO/F-Hkust solutions and electrospinning parameters were summarized in Table 1.
Eventually, in order to crosslink pectin nanofibers dualcrosslinking method with ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ and glutaraldehyde (GLU) was applied. First, the electrospun nanofibers were exposed to glutaraldehyde and HCl vapour (with 10:1 ratio) in a sealed desiccator and incubated at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ [49]. After that, Pectin nanofibers were crosslinked with $\mathsf{C a C l}_{2}$ at room temperature as reported in previous articles [3,19,50]. After immersion of covalent-crosslinked nanofibers in ethanol for $5~\mathrm{min}$ , they were pre-crosslinked in $95\%$ ethanol containing $2\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathsf{C a C l}_{2}$ for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Then, the nanofibers were crosslinked in a $40\mathrm{wt\%}$ aqueous $\mathsf{C a C l}_{2}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Afterwards, the crosslinked nanofibers were rinsed with glycine solution, excess DI and phosphate buffered saline (PBS) to remove unreacted GLU and $\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$ .
## 2.7. Pectin/PEO/F-Hkust characterization
## 2.7.1. Physical and chemical characterization
The SEM images of the pectin/PEO/F-Hkust nanofibers were captured by a Bruker XFlash 6 l10 microscope (Germany). The average diameters of the 50 nanofibers were reported and F-Hkust nanoparticles were also detected in nanofibrous mats by energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX). ATR-FTIR spectroscopy of pectin/PEO/F-Hkust nanofibers mats were conducted using a FTIR spectrometer (PerkinElmer, Norwalk, CT). As mentioned in Section 2.5.2, mechanical properties of the pectin/PEO/F-Hkust nanofibrous mats also were characterized to calculate the E, S. σ, σ and ε as it was done for pectin/PEO mats [48].
## 2.7.2. Cu ion release study
To study copper release from pectin/F-Hkust mats, $2\mathrm{mm}^{2}$ (approximately $2~\mathrm{mg}$ ) samples were immersed in PBS solution at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , and samples taken after 1,3,5,7, and 14 days. Inductively coupled plasma atomic emission (ICP-AES) spectrometer (Sequential type, Shimadzu, Japan) was utilized to assess the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ concentration in each sample. F-Hkust and pure pectin nanofibrous mat were used as controls.
## 2.7.3. Folic acid release behavior study
Two $\mathrm{mm}^{2}$ (or approximately $2\mathrm{mg}$ ) of each samples were added into a dialysis membrane, and placed in the release medium containing $4\mathrm{mL}$ PBS $\mathrm{\Phi^{\prime}p H}=7.4,1\mathrm{M}$ ). Then the release medium containers were put into incubator-shaker at constant temperature of $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ Aliquots were collected from outside the dialysis tubing at determined time intervals in which the taken out volume of medium was refreshed with fresh release medium of the same volume. UV–Visible absorption spectroscopy (Epoch Nanodrop) along with a folic acid calibration curve was used to detect the concentration of folic acid. The corrected drug concentrations were calculated by following equation reported by Klimundová et al. [51]:
$$
C_{\mathrm{n,c}}=C_{\mathrm{n,m}}-\left(\frac{V_{d}}{V_{t}}\times C_{(n-1),m}\right)
$$
where $C_{n,m},V_{t}$ and $V_{d}$ are the measured concentration of n-sample, volume of receptor and volume of liquid taken out, respectively.
## 2.7.4. Cell culture
L929 fibroblast cells were cultured in Dulbecco's Modification of Eagles Medium (DMEM, low glucose; Sigma, USA) complemented with $10\%$ v/v fetal bovine serum (FBS; Sigma, USA), $1\%\mathrm{v/v}$ Pen-Strep (100 $\mathrm{\DeltaU\mL^{-1}}$ Penicillin and $100~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ Streptomycin; Sigma–Aldrich, USA). Then the culture medium was incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C};$ Incubation conditions were the humidified atmosphere containing $5\%\thinspace{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}$ . The medium was refreshed every 2 days.
## 2.7.5. Cell toxicity assessment by MTT assay
The samples were immersed in $70\%$ ethanol and rinsed with PBS three times (each time last $10\mathrm{min}$ ) to be sterilized [16,49,50,52]. Then composite nanofibrous mats were extracted according to ISO-10993-
12 standard in DMEM under sterile conditions [53]. The extractions then added to each well for cytotoxicity evaluation which was indirectly investigated by 3-[4, 5-dimethylthiazol-2-yl]-2, 5-diphenyltetrazolium bromide; thiazolyl blue (MTT) assay. One mL L929-cell suspension ( $1\times104$ cell per well) were seeded in each well and kept in an incubator under $5\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 1, 3, and 5 days (Stat Fax-2100; GMI, USA). The culture medium changes were done every 2 days. At determined times, $100\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $5~\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ MTT solution was added to each well after the culture medium was removed. The solution was incubated at $37^{\circ}C$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ followed by mixing with $50\upmu\mathrm{L}$ DMSO to dissolve formazan crystals. After 10-minute incubation at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the concentrations of dissolved salts were measured by absorbance measuring at $545\mathrm{nm}$ using an ELISA micro-plate reader (Stat Fax- 2100, USA). Each sample was tested for three times and the percentage of cell viability was calculated by below equation:
$$
\mathrm{Cell\viability\%}=\left({\frac{\mathrm{sample/s\average\OD}}{\mathrm{Control/s\average\OD}}}\right)\times100
$$
## 2.7.6. Antibacterial activity assessment
Antibacterial property of the F-Hkust nanoMOFs, pristine Pectin/PEO mats, and F-Hkust nanocomposite mats were investigated by the spread plate technique (colony formation counting method). Staphylococcus aureus (S. aureus, ATCC 12600) and Escherichia coli (E. coli, ATCC 9637) were selected (representing Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria, respectively) and cultured into $8\mathrm{cm}$ petri dishes containing sterile Mueller-Hinton nutrient agar under a laminar hood condition. The sterilized samples were cut into small pieces weighing $10~\mathrm{mg}$ and placed into each well of 24-well plate. The well containing bacteria solution with no sample was considered as negative control and the well containing only PBS was used as positive control. Then $500\upmu\mathrm{L}$ suspension of bacteria in PBS were added to each well shake-incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}.$ . After that, $10\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of bacterial solution was drawn from each well and diluted 10 times with PBS. Then $30~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of the solution was spread onto nutrient agar petri dishes and incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for other $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Finally, the number of bacterial colony forming units (CFUs) were counted and photographed as the after-culture bacteria count. The Antibacterial ratio (or reduction of bacteria) was then calculated and averaged as follows:
Antibacterial ratio $(\%)=\frac{\mathsf{A}-\mathsf{B}}{\mathsf{A}}\times100\$ where A and B are the mean number of bacteria in the control samples and the treated samples respectively, after $24\mathrm{h}$ incubation [54,55].
## 2.8. Statistical analysis
All data of experiments were reported as mean $\pm$ standard deviation (SD). The statistical analysis to compare any significant difference between the groups was conducted by two way analysis of variance (ANOVA). A confidence level of $95\%$ ( $p<0.05\$ ) was considered to indicate statistical significance.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Cu-based MOFs
The size and morphology of Hkust-1 and F-Hkust are shown in Fig. 2. Hkust-1 was observed with sharp edge, and rhombic dodecahedron morphology, while F-Hkust is more likely rounded rather than rhombic-shaped. In addition, particle size and morphology of F-Hkust proved that we could reduce the size of Cu-MOF from $1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ to ${<}200\ \mathrm{nm}$ by introducing folic acid. The number distribution of FHkust sizes measured by DLS in colloidal aqueous dispersion revealed a hydrodynamic diameter of $217.3\pm34.4\:\mathrm{nm}$ . The zeta potential of the original Hkust-1 and F-Hkust MOFs was $-11.9$ and $-13.6\ensuremath{\mathrm{mV}}$ respectively. The zeta potential of F-Hkust MOF was lower than the Hkust-1 crystals due to the presence of negatively charged carboxyl groups on the MOF’s surface by folic acid modification.
The chemical compositions of MOF specimens were examined using FTIR technique and the FTIR spectra (Fig. 3). Considering the curve of Hkust-1, the absorption peaks at 1647 and $1562~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are related to the $\mathtt{C O O^{-}}$ . In addition, the absorption peaks at 1375, 1445, 731, and $716~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were assigned to $\mathsf{C}{\=}{\=}{\mathsf{C}}$ of benzene ring. The peaks at 1105 and $940~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were contributed to aromatic ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{H}}.$ There is a specific band at $488\thinspace{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ related to Cu\ O vibrations indicating the linkage between copper and organic ligand [42,56,57].
In F-Hkust curve, there is $\mathrm{CH}-\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ bond at $2916\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The peaks attributed to $\mathsf{C}{\=}{\=}{\mathsf{C}}$ vibrations of benzene ring also observed in this curve transferring with slight changes (to 1449, 1371, 762, and $728\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ , which can be related to the formation of hydrogen bonds between the folic acid and the amine in the Hkust-1. The characterized peaks at 928 and $456~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were related to the aromatic ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{H}}$ and $\mathtt{C u\mathrm{-}0}$ vibrations, respectively. After modification of Hkust-1 by adding folic acid, a band assigned to $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ of amide I in Pterin ring appeared at 1590 and $1647\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The peak at $3322\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ related to N\ H of amide could be clearly observed certifies the presence of folic acid [42]. These FTIR results confirmed the presence of folic acid in F-Hkust.
Fig. 3 represents the XRD patterns of prepared MOFs. Consistent with literature, the XRD data confirmed that the crystal structure of FHkust was quite similar to that of Hkust-1 demonstrating that Folic acid can be grafted without a loss of structural integrity but with some trivial alterations in the Bragg intensities [42,58]. The Hkust-1 unit cell have a FCC structure with the respective hkl planes at 2θ values of 6.7 (200), 9.4 (220), 11.6 (222), 13.4 (400), 16.4 (420), 17.5 (333), 19.1 (440), 26.01 (553), 29.4 (751) and 35.4 (951) [59–63]. The diffraction peaks of the synthesized Hkust-1 are consistent with those in the simulated MOF-199 pattern based on the published crystal CIF file via Maud software [64–66].
## 3.2. Pectin/PEO nanofibrous mats
## 3.2.1. Electrospinning of pectin/PEO blends
Electrospinning of pure aqueous pectin solution may be the most environment-friendly way to produce pectin nanofibers [11]. However, electrospinning pectin still faces challenges mainly because of its polyelectrolyte nature in solution [6,16,67] and/or its weakly gelled character with clear yield stress [68]. The repulsive force between ionic groups on the polymer backbone is expected to hinder the formation of sufficient chain entanglements to allow continuous fiber formation during jet stretching, whipping and bending, generally resulting in formation of nanobeads instead of nanofibers [24]. To overcome the challenge of electrospinning pectin, the most successful and easiest method is blending it with carrier polymers (e.g. PEO and/or polyvinyl alcohol (PVA)) [17]. The blending can be promoted by applying surfactants (e.g. Triton X-100, pluronic F127, and lecithin) and/or cosolvents (e.g. dimethyl sulfoxide and glycerol) to the pectin solution [26]. These co-spinning agents are all biocompatible and biodegradable and will not constraint the final applications of pectin-based nanofibers [6,24]. Therefore, inspired by previous works [10,18], PEO and Triton X-100 (a non-ionic surfactant) were incorporated in this study to improve jet stability during electrospinning mainly caused by the reduction of electric conductivity and surface tension of the polymer solution, respectively [8]. Solutions of different pectin/PEO ratios with various MWs of PEOs were prepared in order to facilitate the electrospinning of nanofibers. According to other studies [11], although PEO is generally considered biocompatible, sometimes it has some minor drawbacks in in vivo applications. So, it was attempted to electrospin pectin nanofibers with at as small amount PEO as possible. Moreover, inclusion of 1 wt% Triton X100 was used as an adjusted percentage enabling formation of nanofibers at pectin/PEO ratio of 75:25 to 85:15. Pectin/PEO blends were prepared with different mass ratios summarized in Table 2.
3.2.2. Effect of Mw of PEO on the morphology of pectin nanofibers Pectin-based nanofibers containing three distinct amounts of PEO with three different Mws of PEO were illustrated in Fig. 4. By increasing the Mw of PEO, the required amount of PEO to produce bead-free nanofibers decreased. In addition, increasing the proportion of PEO resulted in increasing the fiber diameter and decreasing the bead formation. Bead-free nanofibers were produced at all three pectin/PEO ratios ranging from 75:25 to 85:15 when $4000~\mathrm{kDa}$ PEO was used. However, the beaded nanofibers were observed in 1000X-4, 1000X-5 and 2000X-5. Fig. 5 represents the range of average fiber diameters as a function of the amount of PEO and its Mw in the electrospinning solution. PEO of higher molecular weight leads to more chain entanglements and consequently improves the jet stability of polysaccharide solution [32]. The thinnest fibers were observed in 1000X-5 nanofibers, approximately $70\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter, whereas the thickest fibers of 4000X3 were $227{\mathrm{nm}}$ in diameter.
## 3.2.3. FTIR result of pectin/PEO nanofibers
FTIR spectroscopy was utilized to characterize the composition of pectin/PEO nanofibers and the possible interaction between citrus pectin and PEO. As shown in Fig. 6, pristine pectin presents two characteristic peaks at 1627 and $1761\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , induced by the C\ O stretching band of carboxyl groups $(-\mathsf{C O O H})$ and methyl-esterified carboxyl groups $(-\mathsf{C O O C H}_{3})$ , respectively [3,11,19,55,69]. Considering the “finger print” region of polysaccharides, Pectin have specific absorption bands in the $1000{-}1200\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ range representing ring vibrations overlapping with the stretching vibrations of C-OH (side groups) and the C-O-C glycosidic bonds [3]. The main peaks at 1016 and $1096\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ attributed to C\ O stretching of the primary alcohol and backbone vibrations, respectively [52,67,70]. Moreover, the absorption peak was observed at $1231~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ which is due to C\ C bonds in the ring structure of pectin molecules [3,69]. PEO shows the peaks in 1235–1250 and $1341~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ which are assigned to $C H_{2}$ twisting and wagging modes [70]. The peaks at 2877 and $828~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are related to H-C-H bending band [55]. All the tested pectin/PEO nanofibers, including 1000X-(3–5), 2000X(3–5) and 4000X-(3–5) displayed all above-mentioned characteristic peaks of citrus pectin and PEO. The peaks at 828, 1240 and $1341~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ( $C H_{2}$ vibrations) can be used to characterize the transplanar conformation of PEO in the fibers [10]. PEO and Triton X-100 have similar $-\mathrm{CH}_{2}.$ – $\mathrm{CH}_{2}{-}0{-}$ repeating units in molecular chains, so the C\ H and C\ O absorbance bands at 2877 and $1607\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ may account for both, but a characteristic band at $1511~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ indicate the presence of Triton X100 attributed to the aromatic ring stretch [12].
## 3.2.4. Tensile mechanical behavior of pectin/PEO nanofibrous mats
As pectin shows weak mechanical stability, crosslinkers such as glutaraldehyde as well as synthetic polymers like PEO [9] were unitized to improve its mechanical properties. Mechanical properties of pectinbased mats composing of different ratios and molecular weights of PEO were measured by static tensile tests. As listed in Table 3, strain or elongation values is indicating that pectin-based mats are moderately flexible and do not elongate ${>}10\%$ . The rigidity of pectin nanofibrous mats could probably be attributed to high degree of orientation of pectin polymer chains during electrospinning.
The mats became stiffer when PEO of the higher molecular weight was involved. The presence of longer molecular chains improves chain entanglement and consequently provides a stronger interaction with both polymers, useful to enhance mechanical performances of pectin/ PEO nanofibrous mats [67,71]. Moreover, in each group of samples composing of PEO with the same molecular weight, the $S.\upsigma$ and $\varepsilon$ increased respectively by increasing PEO content suggesting the formation of tightly hydrogen bonded networks between polymers and Triton X100 [71]. The strongest mat was the mat having $25\%$ of PEO ( $\mathrm{Mw}=$ $4000~\mathrm{kDa}^{\cdot}$ ) which showed a strength value of $0.02789\pm0.00506\mathrm{~N~}$ $\mathrm{tex}^{-1}$ . This mat also has approximately $8\%$ elongation.
## 3.3. Pectin/PEO/F-Hkust nanocomposite mats
## 3.3.1. SEM/EDX
According to FE-SEM micrographs and mechanical tensile behaviors of the nanofibrous scaffolds, the pectin nanofibers containing $25\mathrm{wt\%}$ of PEO with molecular weight of $4000\mathrm{kDa}$ selected as a best fitted nanofibers in which F-Hkust MOFs can be incorporated in order to fabricate improved nanocomposite mats. SEM and ESEM/EDX results of pectin/ PEO/F-Hkust nanofibers are represented in Fig. 7 in which inclusion of $2\mathrm{wt\%}$ of F-Hkust could be clearly observed due to formation of beaded nanofibers. After study of the effect of F-Hkust on nanofibers morphology, it was observed that the average diameter of nanofibers was decreased by increasing the MOF percentage from $211.6\ \pm$ $3.8~\mathrm{nm}$ in FK-4000 mat to $162.8\pm4.1~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and $132.7\pm3.6\$ in FK4000-1 and FK-4000-2 respectively. The reason could be due to solution conductivity, which increased by more free ions in solution. Consequently, it causes thinner fiber diameters [8,28]. From EDX images in Fig. 7, red dots attributed to Cu, confirms presence and the upward trend of F-Hkust concentration in nanofibers. Fig. 7 also exhibits desirable dispersion of Cu in pectin/PEO/F-Hkust nanofibrous samples.
## 3.3.2. FTIR result
The FTIR spectra of pectin/PEO/F-Hkust samples are shown in Fig. 8 displaying all mentioned characteristic peaks of citrus pectin, PEO, and consequently illustrating the absorption peaks of pectin/PEO mats. These characteristic peaks are at 2884, 1744–1754, and $1620~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ assigned to vibrations of O\ H, CH, $\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ –CO of the methyl ester group $\begin{array}{r l}{\lefteqn{\big(-\mathsf{C O O C H}_{3},}}\end{array}$ and the carbonyl group of the carboxylate ion $(\mathsf{C O O}^{-})$ , respectively. There is a weaker absorption peak at $1462~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ associated to symmetric stretching of the carboxylate ion [14,20,69,72]. The noted peak at $842~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is also related to H-C-H bending band of PEO [10,11]. In addition, the spectrum of pectin/PEO/ F-Hkust nanofibers shows peaks between 800 and $1360\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ which is the “finger print” region and is specific for each compound due to the stretching vibrations of C-O-C and $c{-}c$ of the glucose ring [14,20,71]. There are slightly shift observed in carbonyl stretching frequencies at $1754~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ of FK-4000 to $1748~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in FK-4000-1 and $1744\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in FK-4000-2. The reason may be due to MOF incorporation in pectin/PEO nanofibers and indicate the formation of hydrogen bonding between MOF and pectin/PEO matrix [25,69,71].
3.3.3. Tensile mechanical behavior pectin/PEO/F-Hkust mats Mechanical parameters of FK-4000, Fk-4000-1, and FK-4000-2 including S.σ, E, σ (or tensile stress at yield), ε, and density were calculated using their stress-strain curves summarized in Table 4. The crosslinking reaction of pectin/PEO mats with ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ ions causes further decrease in tensile specific strength. As a matter of fact, second step of crosslinking reaction is occurred in an aqueous solution containing a crosslinking agent. So it is likely that some water is absorbed and entangled in the interchain space in the network which can as plasticizer [20,71,73,74]. In general, MOF nanoparticles have several carboxylic acid functional groups to interact with polymer matrix (including carboxyl and hydroxyl groups) [48,49,75,76]. Also, MOFs have large surface area which can provide high stress transfer [15]. Accordingly, they can effectively absorb, disperse, and dissipate the applied load of polymeric matrix during a mechanical rapture [77]. Therefore, this composite mats exhibits substantially improved mechanical performance compared with pristine pectin/PEO mats. The tensile strength of pectin-based mats increased from 18.98 to $52.25\ensuremath{\mathrm{MPa}}$ by increasing the F-Hkust content from 0 to $2\mathrm{wt\%}$ 1 $\dot{p}<0.05\dot{}$ . The highest Young's Modulus, calculated slope of the linear segment of the stress- strain curves of FK-4000-2 was measured about 19.13 MPa meaning more MOFs in structure cause stronger mats. The Young's modulus enhancements of composite mats (containing F-Hkust) in comparison with FK-4000, indicating the existence of inter-molecular interactions (hydrogen and electrostatic bonds) between F-Hkust and polymers [25] and the much higher rigidity of MOF nanoparticles compared to pectin [22]. In contrast, elongation at break which displays material flexibility decreased from 13.37 to $6.67\%$ . It is also noteworthy that upon increasing the F-Hkust content from 1 to $2\mathrm{wt\%}$ the decrease in elongation at break was not significant ( $\dot{p}$ -value $=0.086>0.05$ ). The reduction of elongation at break may be due to restriction of free segmental mobility of the pectin chains because of existed interactions between pectin and F-Hkust [15,16,22]. As expected, the increasing trend was also observed for tensile specific strengths by increasing the concentration of F-Hkusts up to $2.0\mathrm{wt\%}$ ( $\dot{p}<0.05\dot{}$ . The reason can be observed in Eq.1, indicating that tensile specific strength of mat is proportional to maximum load endured before rapture but reversely correlated with density [48]. Density remarkably decreases by incorporation of nanoMOFs which is obvious because MOFs have highly porous structures. These results are consistent with the previous mechanical properties findings [15,25].
