## 3.3.5. Cytotoxicity of pectin/PEO/F-Hkust mats by MTT assay
The F-Hkust was selected because Xia et al. had determined the cytotoxicity of Hkust-1 and F-Hkust by MTT and lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) methods towards both human epithelial keratinocytes (HEKas) and human dermal fibroblasts (HDFs). They also conducted Scratch assay, Endothelial Tubule Formation assay and Wound Healing in vivo assay. Considering these experiments, they reported that F-Hkust exhibited much lower toxicity compared with Hkust-1. Moreover, they declared that not only there was no cytotoxicity due to folic acid at concentrations below $0.5\mathrm{mM}$ but also ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ ions stimulate keratinocyte and fibroblast migration through the up regulation of several growth factors. They concluded that F-Hkust increased HDF migration due to the combination of low cytotoxicity together with the sustained release of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and folic acid [34]. Therefore, in this study, in vitro cell viability of FK-4000, FK-4000-1, and FK-4000-2 were investigated. The MTT assay was carried out after incubation of different mats for 1, 3, and 7 days. As can be seen in Fig. 11, L929 viabilities of the samples are lower than control samples. However, the cell viability of lower than $70\%$ compared to the non-treated group is considered cytotoxic in agreement with ISO 10993-5 [3,16,53,89]. As a result, all fabricated samples were considered biocompatible towards L929 fibroblast cells since all survival rates in MTT experiments approximately exceed $80\%$ . The biocompatibility of Pectin-based porous materials is in line with other studies. Maccune et al. indicated that pectin-based nanofibers did not show significant cytotoxicity towards MC3T3-E1 cells after $^{72\mathrm{h}}$ incubation, based on the LDH assay [17]. Kulikouskaya et al. claimed that scaffolds based on pectin possessed effective cell-scaffold interaction proved by elongated large focal contacts formation [90]. Likewise, the viability of L929 fibroblast cells after $24\mathrm{h}$ incubation in the leachates of the gelatin-cross-linked pectin nanofibers of different concentrations were displayed around $100\%$ by Shi et al. [91]. Nevertheless, negligible reduction of biocompatibility may be due to the release of residual unreacted reactants from the final macromolecule composition in the medium or acidic pH of the hydrogels. In addition, carboxyl groups of F-Hkust as a hydrophilic agent can help to enhance the solubility of pectin/PEO/F-Hkust mats and subsequently, increases their internalization into fibroblasts resulting in cell death [49]. These results suggest that pectin/PEO/F-Hkust mats are promising biomaterials from a biocompatibility point of view.
## 3.3.6. Cu ion release profile of pectin/PEO/F-Hkust mats
Generally, copper plasma concentration is at the level of $0.6{-}1.6~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ (or $\mathrm{mg~L}^{-1}$ ) in the healthy humans [92–94]. Releases of $\mathsf{C u}^{+2}$ in PBS medium from F-Hkust and nanofibrous samples containing MOF were investigated after 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7 and 9 days displaying that release rates were dependent on the copper contents of the samples. As shown in Fig. 12, F-Hkust sample has an extreme burst release in the first $24\mathrm{h}$ while the burst release of copper ions alleviate by incorporating them in FK-4000-1 and FK4000-2 nanofibers. Controlled and prolonged release of $\mathsf{C u}^{+2}$ ions could be accounted as another advantage of utilizing pectin nanofibers as carriers of nanoMOFs. Although $\mathsf{C u}^{+2}$ ion concentrations increased after 9 days leading to prolonged release duration and long-acting antibacterial activity, the cumulative release of copper after 9 days in all cases were less than accepted dose indicating that cell toxicity induced by copper ions is less possible.
## 3.3.7. Folic acid release profile of pectin/PEO/F-Hkust mats
Eight solutions with concentrations between $1\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ and $25\upmu\up g$ $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ were prepared and measured by UV–visible spectrophotometry solution using the phosphate buffer saline solution as blank at physiological pH of around 7.4. The absorbance was measured at $298\mathrm{nm}$ indicating a linear calibration curve $\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.9936\$ ) which relates absorbance and Folic acid concentrations.
The in vitro folic acid release profiles from F-Hkust and pectin-based mats were studied over a period of $164\mathrm{h}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ As shown in Fig. 13, release of the folic acid from F-Hkust nanoparticles was relatively fast during the first $44\mathrm{h}$ in exposure to PBS, and afterward slowed down and eventually reached its maximum at $164\mathrm{h}$ (approximately 7 days). An initial burst release of F-Hkust nanoparticles (approximately $36\upmu\mathrm{g}$ $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ ) within first $24\mathrm{~h~}$ reduced in case of F-Hkust containing mats (FK-4000-1 and FK-4000-2) which can be probably due to entrapment of drug into mats which penetrates to near the surface of nanofibers and consequently to the medium. After the primary phase, release rate of folic acid became slower till the release profile turns into flat line. Generally, controlled drug release from FK-4000-1 and FK-4000-2 was achieved due to advantages of the delivery nanofibers and covalent bonding of folic acid in F-Hkust structure. There is no amount of released drug from FK-4000 having no F-Hkust.
## 4. Conclusion
Composite nanofibers of pectin/PEO blends containing modified copper-based MOFs (F-Hkust) were successfully fabricated through simple one-step electrospinning technique in order to improve controlled drug release and meanwhile reduce cytotoxic effect of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ ions. MOF-199 were first produced and modified by folic acid to improve minimize its drawbacks such as poor stability and decrease their size in nano scale as confirmed by FESEM and DLS analysis. Then, the mechanical and morphological properties of pectin-based nanofibers mats of different proportion and molecular weight of carrier polymer (PEO) were studied to choose the optimum pectin nanofibers as a matric of the final nanocomposite. The FTIR and EDX analysis confirmed that F-Hkust was detected in the pectin/PEO nanofibers as a drug carrier as well as an antibacterial agent. Release studies revealed that nanocomposite structure could effectively reduce the burst effect of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and folic acid from the nanofibers. The antibacterial experiments conformed that the pectin/PEO/F-Hkust nanofibers exhibited antibacterial activity against microbial strains of Staphylococcus aureus and Escherichia coli. The MTT assay results show that all of the samples were biocompatible indicating that the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ ions release are in the safe range. Based on the tensile strength test results, antibacterial, and drug release study, it was concluded that pectin nanofibers containing 2 wt% F Hkust are the most suitable sample comparing to the other samples examined. This study shows a promising initial step in the development of a drug delivery system that can be used in biomedical application.
